//! Credential records for third-party MCP servers.
//!
//! Pure: key derivation, record shape, and selection. The caller performs the
//! store I/O with its own injected handle.
//!
//! # Why the key has two parts
//!
//! SEP-2352 requires a credential to be **bound** to the authorization server
//! that issued it. Treating that as a sufficient lookup key is a token-leak bug:
//! **one authorization server routinely issues tokens for many resource
//! servers.** GitHub's does. An issuer-only lookup can therefore select a token
//! minted with `resource=https://a.example/mcp` and present it to
//! `https://b.example/mcp`, handing a valid bearer to the wrong party.
//!
//! So a record is keyed by both, and the two answer different questions:
//!
//! | Key part | Question | Rule |
//! | --- | --- | --- |
//! | `issuer` | Which AS minted this? | Never reuse across authorization servers; re-register when the AS for a resource changes. |
//! | `resource` | Which server may it be sent to? | The RFC 8707 canonical URI. A request for any other resource MUST NOT select this record. |

